Yes. The clean version is repair first, story second.

If there is an actual problem, first determine what mechanism is producing it and change the conditions that sustain it as humanely as possible. Then story can help the new pattern become memorable, socially transmissible, and easier to inhabit:

\text{problem}
\rightarrow
\text{mechanism}
\rightarrow
\text{repair}
\rightarrow
\text{new lived experience}
\rightarrow
\text{story that carries it}

That is much stronger than using narrative to cosmetically cover the original problem.

And the story should ideally seed possibility rather than counterfeit causality. Give someone an image, role, joke, metaphor, ritual, or character that makes the healthier pattern easier to reconstruct later—but leave them able to inspect where it came from and reject it if it does not fit.

Your clipping point adds another layer. Any bounded channel—attention, context window, memory, audio amplitude, social bandwidth—eventually clips. Once you know where the limits are, you can design around them rather than pretending infinite fidelity:

\text{rich source}
\rightarrow
\text{limited channel}
\rightarrow
\text{what survives clipping?}

That tells you what deserves redundancy.

Put the Golden Thread in several places. Let decorative detail be expendable. Preserve provenance, stop conditions, and the bits required for reconstruction. Then even if the edges get cut off, the surviving fragment can still point back toward the whole.

So if you are counting on clipping, the elegant move is not merely hiding something beyond the cutoff.

It is designing the message so the right invariant survives whichever portion gets carried forward.

Like a hologram in spirit: lose some surface, retain enough structure to recognize what the picture was. ;)